course: medicine
lesson: 74
title: Ice on ponds and cold water in winter
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Freddie in Yorkshire stamps on the edge of the frozen pond, «It's solid, our kid, I'm going out to the middle», but ice on ponds, lakes and canals is often thinner in places you can't see, especially in the middle, near reeds and where streams flow in, and it can break with no warning.
explain: Under the ice the water is only about 4 °C, and in water that cold the body gasps, then hands and legs stop working within minutes; people, children too, die on UK ice every winter, so the rule is simple: never go onto frozen ponds, lakes, rivers or canals.
explain: The deadliest moment is the rescue: people drown going onto ice after a friend or a dog, and dogs usually scramble out by themselves, so you stay on the bank, call 999, shout to them to keep still, and reach with a long branch or throw something that floats, never stepping onto the ice.
explain: Water pulls heat from your body about 25 times faster than air at the same temperature, and wet clothes keep pulling, so Ben, soaked to the knees on a frosty walk, was shivering hard and mumbling half an hour later: that's the body getting dangerously cold (hypothermia).
explain: «Nesh» in Yorkshire and the Midlands means soft about the cold, but cold water isn't about being tough: get out, get dry and warm, and tell an adult, skate at an ice rink, and sledge on slopes that end far from water and roads.
fact: Ice on ponds, lakes, rivers and canals can be thick in one spot and thin a few steps away, and you can't see how thick it is.
fact: Ice is often thinner in the middle, near reeds, near streams flowing in and over springs.
fact: The water under the ice is about 4 °C, more than 30 °C colder than your body.
fact: The advice of RoSPA (the Royal Society for the Prevention of Accidents) is never to go onto ice on ponds, lakes, rivers or canals, and people die every winter doing it.
fact: People drown going onto ice to rescue dogs, and dogs usually manage to get out by themselves.
fact: If someone goes through the ice: stay off it, call 999, tell them to keep still, and reach or throw something from the bank.
fact: Water pulls heat out of the body about 25 times faster than air at the same temperature.
fact: In cold water the hands and legs stop working within minutes, however tough you are.
fact: Strong shivering, slurred words and clumsiness are warning signs that the body is getting dangerously cold, called hypothermia, and when it gets worse you stop noticing the cold.
fact: Canals are often deeper than a child is tall, with steep sides and rubbish frozen in the ice.
fact: UK winter sea is about 8 °C; organised winter dips have lifeguards, and children, being smaller, lose heat even faster than adults, so they shouldn't go into winter water.
fact: Sledges are hard to stop on packed snow and ice, so a slope that runs out onto water or a road is a trap.
fact: The 1963 «Big Freeze» was one of the coldest UK winters on record; ice today is usually much thinner, and people drowned on ice then too.
fact: «Nesh» is Yorkshire and Midlands for feeling the cold easily; «sound» in the North can mean fine; «our kid» means a brother or close family member.
exercise: Freddie's pond edge is solid. Is the middle safe? | No, ice can be thinner where you can't see | Yes, solid at the edge means solid everywhere | Yes, if it holds a stone | No, but only on sunny days
exercise: Where is pond ice often thinnest? | In the middle, near reeds and inflowing streams | Right by the bank where it's shallow | Wherever there's snow on top | Under the ducks
exercise: What's RoSPA's advice about ice on ponds and lakes? | Never go on it | Go on it only in January | Test it with one foot first | Only go out with a friend
exercise: About how cold is the water under the ice? | About 4 °C | About 37 °C | About 20 °C | About 40 °C
exercise: Maisie's dog runs onto the cracking ice. What should she do? | Stay on the bank, call the dog and get help | Run out and carry the dog back | Throw stones to scare it back | Crawl out on her tummy to fetch it back quickly
exercise: Fionn goes through the ice. What should Rory do? | Stay off it, call 999, reach or throw from the bank | Run onto the ice and pull him out | Jump in to keep him warm | Walk away so he isn't blamed
exercise: How much faster does water pull heat from you than air? | About 25 times | About 2 times | No faster at all | About 1,000 times
exercise: Tom's brother says «stop being nesh» after Tom falls in an icy stream. What's true? | Cold water stops muscles working, however tough you are | Tough kids don't feel cold water | Being nesh is what makes water cold | Cold water is just uncomfortable
exercise: Hamza wants to skate along the frozen canal. What's the rule? | Never go on canal ice; skate at a rink | Skate only near the lock, where it's thicker | Skate if it has snow on it | Skate if your mates go first
exercise: Olivia's welly says the ice by the bank is thick. What does that tell her about the middle? | Nothing; ice changes thickness across the pond | It's thick all over | It's twice as thick in the middle, where it's deeper | It's just as thick everywhere
exercise: Ellie wants to join her uncle's New Year sea dip. What's the advice? | Cheer from the beach; winter water isn't for kids | Go in if you're not a wimp | Go in without telling anyone | Swim out further than him
exercise: Ben is shivering hard and mumbling in wet jeans. What is happening? | His body is getting dangerously cold | He's just bored of the long walk home | He has caught a nasty cold from the frost | His wet jeans have shrunk and are too tight
exercise: What should Ben do? | Tell an adult and get dry and warm | Keep playing hard to warm himself up | Take a nap on the grass to save energy | Jump in the stream again to get used to it
exercise: Freya's sledging hill ends at a frozen pond. What's the rule? | Pick a slope that ends far from water and roads | Drag your feet to stop well before you reach the pond | Sledge only at night | Steer round the pond at the bottom
exercise: Grandad skated on the lake in 1963. Is it safe now? | No, that was a rare freeze and lakes were risky then too | Yes, if Grandad did it, it's safe | Yes, ice is thicker these days | Only on the same lake
exercise: The 1963 Big Freeze was how many years before 2026? | 63 | 36 | 1963 | 73
test: «if it holds a stone it'll hold me». Right? | No, a stone weighs a tiny fraction of a child | Yes, stones are much heavier than kids for their size | Yes, if you throw the stone hard | No, but it will hold two kids
test: Maisie: «Biscuit! I'm coming!» What would likely happen if she went out? | Two in the water instead of one | She'd carry Biscuit back safely | The ice would get thicker under her | The dog would sink and she'd be fine
test: Rory: «hold on, I'm coming to get you!» What's the better plan? | Stay on the bank, call 999 and reach or throw | Run out, the ice held Fionn at first | Lie flat on the ice and crawl out to him slowly | Jump in after him to help him swim
test: The cold water shock gasping lasts about 90 seconds. What should Rory do during it? | Keep shouting to Fionn while he calls 999 | Go home quickly and get a towel and blanket | Wait quietly on the bank until it's all over | Step onto the ice to listen for Fionn better
test: Ben: «sound (fine), I'm not even cold». Half an hour later he mumbles. Why can't he tell? | When the body gets too cold you stop noticing | He's lying to look tough | Wet jeans warm you up | Shivering means you're warming up nicely again
test: Hamza: «it's like an ice rink, innit». What's the difference from a rink? | Canal ice is thin, with deep cold water below | There's no difference at all | Rinks are colder than canals, so canals are safer | Canals are shallower than a bath
test: «Can I come in with you? I'm not a wimp». What's true for kids in the January sea? | Kids lose heat even faster than adults | Kids are better at cold water than adults | Being brave keeps you warm | Sea water is warm in winter
test: In a cartoon a penguin and a boy skate across a frozen lake, the ice cracks and they hop to safety. What's real? | People drown when ice breaks; never go on it | You can always hop to safety if you're quick enough | Cracks only appear in cartoons | Lake ice never breaks in winter
test: Freya: «we'll stop way before the pond, sure we will». Why might she not? | Sledges are hard to stop on packed snow and ice | Sledges always stop on their own | She can steer like a car | Ponds pull sledges towards them as they go downhill
test: 1 minute in cold water cools you like about how long in air of the same temperature? | About 25 minutes | About 1 minute, the same | About 5 seconds only | About 25 hours or more
test: Lily: «Grandad did it in '63, so it's fine». What would happen if she tried? | She could fall through much thinner ice | She'd be fine; ice never changes over the years | The lake would freeze thicker for her | Grandad's old skates would keep her up
exam: Identify the recommendation of the Royal Society for the Prevention of Accidents in respect of ice upon inland waters. | No person is to venture onto such ice | Ice may be crossed if it supports a stone | Ice may be crossed in pairs | Ice may be crossed in the month of January
exam: It is given that a companion has fallen through ice. State the appropriate course of action. | Remain upon the bank, summon assistance and reach or throw from safety | Proceed onto the ice to effect a rescue | Enter the water to provide warmth | Depart the scene without informing any person
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the thickness of ice upon a pond? | It may vary considerably and cannot be ascertained by sight | It is uniform throughout the pond | It is greatest at the centre of the pond | It is reliably indicated by the thickness at the bank
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the loss of heat in water. | Water removes heat approximately twenty-five times faster than air | Water removes heat more slowly than air | Water and air remove heat at an identical rate | Water adds heat to the body in winter
